The error in your screenshot is a Docker problem, not a Kubernetes problem.

Error
Plain Text
docker-proxy: no such file or directory
failed to start userland proxy

This usually happens when the Docker installation is corrupted or incomplete.

1. Check Docker Status
Shell
sudo systemctl status docker
Shell
docker version
2. Verify docker-proxy Exists
Shell
which docker-proxy

or

Shell
ls -l /usr/libexec/docker/docker-proxy

or

Shell
find /usr -name docker-proxy

If nothing is returned, Docker is missing the proxy binary.

3. Reinstall Docker

On Ubuntu:

Shell
sudo apt remove docker.io docker-ce docker-ce-cli -y
sudo apt autoremove -y

Install again:

Shell
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker.io -y

Restart:

Shell
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl restart docker

Verify:

Shell
docker run hello-world
4. Delete and Recreate Minikube

After Docker is fixed:

Shell
minikube delete

Start again:

Shell
minikube start --driver=docker

Check:

Shell
kubectl get nodes

Expected:

Plain Text
minikube Ready
5. If Using GitHub Codespaces

I noticed your prompt contains:

Plain Text
/workspaces/ubu1/devmaven1

This looks like a GitHub Codespace.

In many Codespaces environments:

Docker-in-Docker is restricted
Minikube does not work reliably
Port mapping may fail
docker-proxy errors are common

Check:

Shell
echo $CODESPACES

If output is:

Plain Text
true

then you're in a Codespace.

For Kubernetes practice, it's better to use:

Ubuntu VM (VirtualBox/VMware)
Azure VM
AWS EC2
Local Ubuntu machine
